 # Depth Regularization
 
-Also called: Depth Constraint, Depth Supervision, Depth Prior
+Also called: Depth-guided optimization, Monocular depth regularization, Disparity smoothing
 
 ## Overview
-Depth Regularization applies a differentiable auxiliary loss term $\mathcal{L}_{reg}$ to constrain the geometric structure of 3D Gaussian primitives during training, typically by aligning rendered depth or disparity maps with monocular depth estimates or smoothness priors [Parent]. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods like [Frequency Regularization](6130-frequency-regularization.md) and [Smoothed 2D Supervision](7213-smoothed-2d-supervision.md) by explicitly targeting the metric depth accuracy and surface completeness, addressing the "hollowness" and geometric degradation common in sparse-view scenarios [S1, unofficial][S4].
+Depth Regularization methods enforce geometric consistency in 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) by adding a differentiable auxiliary loss term $\mathcal{L}_{reg}$ that penalizes deviations between rendered depth/disparity maps and external depth estimates (e.g., monocular predictions or sensor data) [Parent]. This family solves the problem of geometry degradation, hollowness, and noisy depth in sparse-view or unstructured capture scenarios where color-only optimization fails to converge to accurate surface positions [S4]. It sets itself apart from siblings like [Frequency Regularization](6130-frequency-regularization.md) (which targets spectral artifacts) and [Smoothed 2D Supervision](7213-smoothed-2d-supervision.md) (which operates on 2D image gradients) by explicitly supervising the 3D spatial structure via depth metrics [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
