+### 2DGS + $\hat{L}_d$ variant
+- **Definition**: A variant of 2D Gaussian Splatting (2DGS) that incorporates a depth regularization strategy by enabling supervision and gradient flow to depths within the CUDA backend rasterizer, supervised with sensor or monocular depth estimates [S5].
+- **Formula**: The overall optimization loss is defined as $L = L_{rgb} + \tilde{L}_d$, where $L_{rgb}$ is the original loss from 2DGS [16] and $\tilde{L}_d$ is the depth regularization term [S5].
 
 ## Background
-Depth regularization in the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting emerged as a solution to the geometric degradation observed in sparse-view reconstruction [S4]. Standard 3DGS, optimized for dense multi-view inputs, often produces "hollow" or incomplete geometry when trained on few shots [S1, unofficial]. Early approaches like Pulsar utilized sphere-based representations with opacity-depth energy terms to guide geometry [S3]. As the field evolved, methods like DNGaussian introduced explicit depth normalization and two-stage regularization (Hard/Soft) to align Gaussians with monocular depth priors [S1, unofficial]. Other works, such as PixelSplat, integrated depth regularization into feed-forward architectures to refine point cloud generation [S2]. Surveys categorize these as "regularization-based methods," contrasting them with "generalizability-based methods" that rely on learned priors or generative models [S4].
+Depth regularization in 3D Gaussian Splatting emerged as a response to the limitations of color-only optimization in sparse-view settings, where geometry often becomes hollow or noisy [S4]. Early sphere-based neural rendering methods like Pulsar used opacity-depth energy terms to guide sphere placement [S3]. In the context of 3DGS, methods like DNGaussian introduced hard and soft depth regularizers to align Gaussian centers with monocular depth estimates, addressing geometry degradation in few-shot scenarios [S1, unofficial]. Edge-aware disparity regularization adapted techniques from stereo vision to preserve depth edges while smoothing flat regions [S2]. General surveys note that regularization-based methods trade higher computation for better geometric quality compared to feed-forward generalizable methods [S4]. Recent variants like 2DGS+$\hat{L}_d$ integrate depth supervision directly into the rasterizer backend for more efficient gradient flow [S5].
 
